## Checkpoint 5: all-M>=4 one-deficit wall exclusion

Proved candidate awaiting independent audit:
`docs/reports/target_c_all_M_one_deficit_sector_peeling_2026-10-07.md`.
The exact sorted contact graph has a directed half-plane rule: removing
either the forward ring edge or outward diagonal at an offaxis site
forces every nonnegative stress weight there to vanish. The boundary-row
vacancy propagates this rule through one sector. For M>=4 the axis has
an interior seed r=2,...,M-2, which empties both its ends; then the same
argument rotates through all sectors. Every moving-involving row vanishes.

Successive half-plane elimination directly constructs strictly opening
velocities, avoiding a floating LP or an extrapolated basis. Inactive
pairs and containment persist at a sufficiently small finite amplitude.
The protected root adds the Nth site, paying an *arbitrary* z=N-1 old
cloud and one wall vertex, with zero new contacts, at least two old contacts
lost and every exterior isolate shielded. This proves the family statement
for every fixed M>=4 and all sufficiently small positive curvature, also
for fixed strictly ordered symmetric rate profiles. No uniform onset in
M is asserted. With the audited Oler bound, only z=N survives in the
specified wall class at this scope.

Exact finite half-plane enumeration for M=4,...,10 eliminates every row;
constructive exact velocity certificates at M=4 and M=6 pass every unit
row. M=3 has no local peeling step; that is a regression for this method,
not a universal impossibility. The M=6 simplex search is now secondary:
the all-M analytic peeling proof supersedes the need for another sample.
Next attacks: independently rederive the finite maximum-population bridge;
seek a chamber transfer not requiring an exact sorting wall; synthesize
the direction/elimination mechanism with paired-cap retained-wall release.
The terminal goal remains active and C-II/Level I remain open.

