## Checkpoint 8: effective Oler chart for arbitrary low-slack unit walls

Proved candidate awaiting independent audit:
`docs/reports/target_c_effective_small_slack_unit_wall_chart_2026-10-07.md`.
For any convex unit wall and full wall-plus-cloud area-form Oler slack
sigma<=1/1000, saturation outside the wall gives a Delaunay triangulation
with circumradii<=1 and no extra interior point. Each triangle has area
at least sqrt(3)/4. An obtuse triangle and its longest-edge neighbour
cost at least (2-sqrt(3))/4 in total excess, so every triangle is acute.
Quantitative edge/angle bounds force all interior valences six and
boundary valences at most three; equilateral development is a complete
convex triangular-lattice polygon with at most six corner turns. Actual
coordinates differ by at most 60*b*sigma from that developed chart.

For the arithmetic six-wall maximum population this yields a uniform
P_M chart and displacement <=1000*M^4*theta for every M>=4 and
0<theta<=1/(10000*M^3). The unspecified compactness onset is removed
in that window; uniform finite-triangle isolation is still required.
Five exact finite sorting examples check 1446 triangle areas/circumradii,
all acute signs, 2070 internal Delaunay conditions, full lower separation
and the quantitative displacement bound. These samples are not the
arbitrary-wall proof.

The missing generic macroscopic hypothesis is explicit: canonical
chambers have total Oler slack O(D), whereas this reconstruction needs
small absolute total slack. Next derive a finite defect budget one point
below equality, then use defect-free regions or collective motion without
losing the exact boundary cardinality. The cap and full-core routes stay
available and the terminal goal remains active.

